import pf.Html
import ../../Ui/Code
import ../../Ui/Heading
import ../../Ui/Nav
import ../../Generated/Sources
import ../../Generated/Paths
import ../../Ui/Button
import ../../Ui/Feature
import ../../Ui/FeatureGrid
import ../../Ui/PageCards
import ../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../Ui/Way

## rocx's overview: what the markup is, why it is shaped the way it is, and its pages, as cards.
meta = {
	title: "rocx: markup in Roc",
	description: "rocx is Roc Solid's markup: Roc with markup wherever an expression can stand. rocx build turns each file into plain Roc with the same line numbers, so the compiler checks every value in a page; the server renders it, and the browser runs the same Roc to make it live.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "why", label: "Why rocx" },
		{ id: "kinds", label: "Pages, components, endpoints" },
		{ id: "build", label: "A file, and the Roc it becomes" },
		{ id: "pages", label: "rocx's pages" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="rocx · Roc Solid's markup"
				title="Write pages and components in Roc. Render them on the server. Run them in the browser."
				actions={[
					<Button href={Paths.syntax} primary>Learn the syntax</Button>,
					<Button href={Paths.components}>Components</Button>,
				]}
			>
				A <code>.rocx</code> file is Roc with markup wherever an expression can go; pages, layouts and
				components are all written this way. <code>rocx build</code> turns it into plain Roc, line for line,
				for the Roc compiler to check. The server renders a page to HTML, and the browser runs the same
				Roc, compiled to WebAssembly, to keep it live.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="why">Why rocx</Heading>
			<FeatureGrid>
				<Feature title="Checked by Roc">
					<p>
						Every <code>{"{hole}"}</code> is a Roc expression the compiler checks: a hole shows text, a
						number or markup; a list hole takes a <code>List(Html)</code>, an attribute a <code>Str</code>,
						a handler a function of the right type. Errors point at your <code>.rocx</code> line, quoting
						your code.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Safe by construction">
					<p>
						<code>Html</code> is opaque: no string can become markup. Text and attribute values are
						escaped, URLs that could run script are replaced, and handlers are Roc functions, never
						script in an attribute (<a href={Paths.safety}>safety</a>).
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Line for line">
					<p>
						Line <var>N</var> of the generated Roc is line <var>N</var> of your file, so compiler
						reports, <code>dbg</code> and crash messages point to the right place, with no source maps to
						break.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Fine-grained, end to end">
					<p>
						As in SolidJS, a component runs once. A change reruns only the holes that read it, each
						patching its own node; nothing is re-rendered to find what changed.
					</p>
				</Feature>
			</FeatureGrid>

			<Heading id="kinds">Pages, components, endpoints</Heading>
			<Way kind="Pages" href={Paths.routing} more="Routing →" code={[<Code source={Sources.hello.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />]}>
				A page is a file, <code>routes/…/+page.rocx</code>, at the URL its folders spell. It is a component
				the server renders for each request, inside the layouts above it, and the browser hydrates. What it
				wraps in <code>{"<NoHydration>"}</code> stays the server's HTML.
			</Way>
			<Way kind="Components" href={Paths.components} more="Components →" code={[<Code source={Sources.home_update.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />]}>
				SolidJS 2.0's model: a component runs once and may make signals, values that change, and the
				holes that read them rerun. A page places a component like an element.
			</Way>
			<Way kind="Endpoints" href={Paths.calls} more="Talking to the server →" code={[<Code source={Sources.way_endpoint.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />]}>
				A route's <code>+server.roc</code> holds its endpoints: a function per HTTP method at a URL, typed
				at both ends. A component reads a GET as a plain value; the server answers it while rendering, and
				the page carries the answer to the browser. The handler runs only on the server and isn't in the
				browser's download.
			</Way>

			<Heading id="build">A file, and the Roc it becomes</Heading>
			<p>
				The counter on the <a href={Paths.root}>front page</a> is this file:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.demo_counter.file} source={Sources.demo_counter.rocx} />
			<p>
				<code>rocx build</code> writes <code>Counter.roc</code> beside it. Its lines 9 to 20, the same
				lines as above, with the markup turned into calls to the platform's <code>Html</code> module:
			</p>
			<Code
				title="Counter.roc, as rocx build writes it"
				source={"view! : { start_value : () => I64 } => Html\nview! = |{ start_value: start_value! }| {\n\t(count!, set_count!) = create_signal!(start_value!())\n\tHtml.element(\"div\", [Html.attr_literal(\"class\", \"counter rx-49lxy6\"), Html.class_of(\"negative\", || count!() < 0),], [\n\t\tHtml.element(\"button\", [Html.attr_literal(\"type\", \"button\"), Html.on(\"click\", || set_count!(count!() - 1)), Html.attr_literal(\"aria-label\", \"One less\"), Html.attr_literal(\"class\", \"rx-49lxy6\"),], [Html.text(\"−\"),]), Html.text(\" \"),\n\t\tHtml.element(\"output\", [Html.attr_literal(\"class\", \"rx-49lxy6\"),], [Html.hole_of(|| Html.hole(count!().to_str())),]), Html.text(\" \"),\n\t\tHtml.element(\"button\", [Html.attr_literal(\"type\", \"button\"), Html.on(\"click\", || set_count!(count!() + 1)), Html.attr_literal(\"aria-label\", \"One more\"), Html.attr_literal(\"class\", \"rx-49lxy6\"),], [Html.text(\"+\"),]), Html.text(\" \"),\n\t\tHtml.element(\"button\", [Html.attr_literal(\"type\", \"button\"), Html.attr_literal(\"class\", \"reset rx-49lxy6\"), Html.on(\"click\", || set_count!(0)), Html.flag_of(\"disabled\", || count!() == 0),], [\n\t\t\tHtml.text(\"Reset\"),\n\t\t]),\n\t])\n}"}
				first={9}
			/>
			<ul>
				<li>
					What calls a <code>!</code> function, such as <code>count!()</code>, can change, so it becomes a
					function the browser reruns: <code>{"{count!().to_str()}"}</code> is
					<code>{"Html.hole_of(|| …)"}</code>, and <code>{"class:negative={…}"}</code> is
					<code>{"Html.class_of(\"negative\", || …)"}</code>. What can't change is written once: an attribute
					from the file is <code>Html.attr_literal</code>, text is <code>Html.text</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					A handler, <code>{"on:click={…}"}</code>, is a Roc function given to <code>Html.on</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					An effectful component's (<code>view!</code>) props become getters: <code>start_value</code> is a
					function, read where the code reads it, so a parent can pass a changing value.
				</li>
				<li>
					The class <code>rx-49lxy6</code> comes from the component's <code>{"<style>"}</code>, which rocx
					scopes to its markup and moves into the app's stylesheet.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				A route file's Roc goes into the app's <code>Generated/</code> folder instead. The
				<a href={Paths.syntax}>syntax</a> page covers everything you can write in markup,
				<a href={Paths.tooling}>tooling</a> the commands, and <a href={Paths.safety}>safety</a> what rocx
				refuses to build, and why.
			</p>

			<Heading id="pages">rocx's pages</Heading>
			<PageCards pages={Nav.pages_of(Nav.rocx_docs).drop_if(|page| page.path == Paths.rocx)} />
		</NoHydration>
	</>
